<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tampico</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tampico is a port city in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>, near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panuco-river">Pánuco River</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Refounded in 1823, it was the scene in 1829 of Santa Anna's defeat of the Spanish expedition under Isidro Barradas, the last Spanish attempt to reconquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. In the early 20th century it became the centre of Mexico's oil boom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tampico.jpg" alt="Tampico" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Comisión Mexicana de Filmaciones from México D. F., México · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tampico,_Centro_Historico_(12059306706).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tampico lies on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panuco-river">Pánuco River</a>, about 10 kilometres inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, in the south-east of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a> and directly north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>. The name comes from the Huastec tam-piko, meaning place of otters or water dogs, a reference to the otters that lived in the rivers and lagoons of the Pánuco delta. The area had several Huastec settlements, among them the important site at Las Flores, which flourished between 1000 and 1250 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1532 the Franciscan priest Andrés de Olmos established a mission and monastery over a former Huastec village, and at his request Spanish officials founded San Luis de Tampico in 1554. That site was abandoned in 1684 because of frequent pirate attacks, and the area stayed empty for nearly 150 years. The present city was founded on 13 April 1823, after Mexican independence. It lived at first from exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and from trade as a market town for an agricultural region. In 1829 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sent troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to regain the region, but General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> forced them to surrender in September.</p><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil well</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was drilled near Tampico, at Ébano, in 1901 by the Californian Edward Doheny, who founded the Mexican Petroleum Company. American investment and a large American community followed, and the Chijol Canal, begun in 1901, carried oil 120 km south through the fields to Tuxpan. The city became the chief oil-exporting port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the second busiest in the world. On 9 April 1914, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, a confrontation between Mexican soldiers and sailors of the USS Dolphin, the Tampico Affair, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> for seven months and helped bring down General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a>. Mexico nationalised its oil industry in 1938.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The oil boom paid for the grand buildings of the downtown, often compared with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. Wrought-iron balconies, some bearing the plaques of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a> forge of Andrew Handyside and Company, line the Plaza de la Libertad, and the neoclassical Town Hall and the English redbrick Customs House stand nearby. The cathedral on the Plaza de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a> dates from the late 19th century, and Doheny gave large sums for its construction after 1902. In 1895 snow was reported to have fallen in Tampico, the farthest south at a coastal location in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tampico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last United States forces left the Mexican port city of Veracruz, which they had occupied since April in response to the <b>Tampico</b> Affair.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-23">23 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Vicente Guerrero abolished slavery in 1829, the year Santa Anna defeated a Spanish reconquest attempt at <b>Tampico</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-mexican-republic">First Mexican Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gulf coast north of <b>Tampico</b> was held by independent peoples such as the Coahuiltecans and resisted Spanish settlement for two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/nuevo-santander">Nuevo Santander</a></li></ul></section>
